In case of international transaction by an assessee with its associated enterprises, section 92C provides the method for computation of tax. It has mentioned several methods and among them is the comparable method. In this method companies having functional similarity are selected for computational purposes. This article mainly discusses on what type of companies are to be excluded in selection of comparables in computing arm's length price with reference to decided case laws. In such cases the findings of the judicial authorities are not generally the precedents but are applicable where these are based on the similarity of facts and circumstances.

INTRODUCTION

1. As persection 92C(1) of the Income-tax Act, 1961 ('Act' for short) the arm's length price in relation to an international transaction or specified domestic transactionshall be determined by any of the following methods, being the most appropriate method, having regard to the nature of transaction or class of transaction or class of associated persons or functions performed by such persons or such other relevant factors as the Board may prescribe, namely :—

comparable uncontrolled price method;

resale price method;

cost plus method;

profit split method;

transactional net margin method;

such other method as may be prescribed by the Board.

Determining arm's length price depends upon comparables of controlled transactions in similar circumstances and thereafter making suitable adjustments to set off the difference to make the transaction commercially comparable. The comparables should be functionally comparable. It is the Transfer Pricing Officer (TPO) who adopts the companies as comparables. The Assessing Officer is to prepare the draft assessment order and to forward the same to the Dispute Resolution Panel ('DRP' for short). The assessee has every right to raise the objections. It is the bounden duty of DRP to consider those objections in accordance with the law. The Assessing Officer is to pass assessment order as per the directions of the DRP. The assessee, if aggrieved against this order, can file an appeal before the Appellate Tribunal.

In this article what types of comparables cannot be selected for the purpose of determining arm's length price has been discussed with reference to the decided case laws.

PRINCIPLES TO BE FOLLOWED IN SELECTING THE COMPARABLES

2. In General Motion India (P.) Ltd. v. Dy. CIT [2014] 146 ITD 559/[2013] 37 taxmann.com 403 (Ahd.), it was held that following principles should be followed in the choice of comparables by the Tribunal -

A comparable cited by the assessee cannot be rejected merely on the ground that the comparable falls outside his jurisdiction on the ground that he could not call for additional information and/or scrutinize the tax record for such period;

Where the DRP sustains the order of TPO in such a case, the matter should be remanded for analyzing each of the comparables and giving reasons for inclusion where there is objection from the assessee or rejection when the assessee relies upon the same.

In Serdia Pharmaceutical (India) (P.) Ltd. v. Asstt. CIT [2011] 44 SOT 391/9 taxmann.com 13 (Mum.) it has been held that there is no bar on taking foreign associated enterprise as a tested party.

FUNCTIONALLY DIFFERENT COMPANIES

3. While selecting the comparable companies the functionally different companies are to be excluded. In Temasek Holdings Advisors (I) (P.) Ltd. v. Dy. CIT [2013] 60 SOT 134/ 38 taxmann.com 80 (Mum.) the assessee, in respect of information technology enabled services with its associated enterprises, selected nine comparables. The TPO rejected seven out of nine as being not comparables. He made his own research for comparables and chose 11 comparables out of which eight were found comparable. His working of profit level indicator was 54.88% as against 21% adopted by the assessee. The Tribunal found that even the eight companies chosen by the TPO were not comparables, since these were either broking companies or merchant bankers or associated management services, with one of them being a credit agency in India and another was engaged in production or distribution with reference to that function and rules, there being no functional similarity when their overall functions were considered. In this context the Tribunal held that the assessee's working and not that of the TPO was found acceptable.

