Transfer Pricing Assessments of captive IT/ITeS entities in India The mark-ups on cost, earned by Indian captive IT/ITeS entities have historically ranged between 10% to 20% 3. These markups have come under strict scrutiny from the revenue authorities in India who have consistently pushed for higher mark-ups despite the routine nature of the functions carried out by GICs.

The benchmarking process adopted by both the taxpayer and the revenue authorities consists of a search carried out on common databases, viz., PROWESS 4 & CAPITALINE PLUS5 for comparable companies, using similar search strings. The set of comparables is further refined using accepted quantitative and qualitative filters. Some of the commonly used quantitative filters are as below:

Companies for which contemporaneous data is not available;

Companies having IT/ITeS revenue less than INR 1 Crore;

Companies having IT/ITeS revenue less than 75% of total revenue/Companies having manufacturing or trading revenue greater than 50%;

Companies having export sales less than 75% of the total revenue;

Companies having employee cost less than 25% of the total revenue;

Companies having onsite revenue greater than 50% of the total revenue;

Companies having related party transactions in excess of 25% of the total revenue.

The remaining comparables are further analysed qualitatively with reference to the information available in the public domain such as Annual Reports, websites etc. to establish functional comparability with the taxpayer. As part of the qualitative analysis, comparables having business models which are different from the taxpayer are excluded. Such comparables generally include:

Companies engaged in high end product development in case of IT comparables;

Companies engaged in Knowledge Process Outsourcing activities in case of ITeS comparables;

Companies incurring significant expenditure on R&D activities in contrast to captive entities which carry out their functions as per the specifications received from the Group;

Companies incurring significant marketing expenditure in contrast to the captive entities which provide their services within the Group and hence do not engaged in business promotion.

Companies providing diversified services for which segmental data is not available

Both the taxpayers and the revenue authorities adopt similar quantitative and qualitative criteria as listed above with some minor variations. The revenue authorities may not analyse R&D expenditure of comparables, but even they agree principally that product development companies are not comparable to captive service providing companies. It is generally the case that companies engaged in product development incur significant R&D expenditure.

Hence, it can be said that the broad approach of both the taxpayers and the revenue authorities in carrying out the benchmarking is similar. However, the revenue ends up with certain very high margin comparables whereas the comparability hinges on the arithmetic mean of the comparables selected. Arithmetic mean being very sensitive to extreme results may be swayed by even a couple of comparables earning very high markups on cost. Based on the perusal of the results of last few years of transfer pricing audit, it can be said that the revenue has been selecting some comparables which are established market leaders developing software products in the IT sector and KPOs in the ITeS sector and earning substantially higher margins on cost. As a result the mean mark-up of the comparables selected by the revenue has shown a tendency to be on a much higher side compared to the mark-ups earned by the captive service providing entities.

